In development"}]]}]}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"Currently being built"}]]}],["$","div","experimental",{"className":"flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"shrink-0","children":["$","span",null,{"title":"Working exploration, not a product","className":"label-mono inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"inline-block h-[6px] w-[6px] rounded-full border border-current bg-transparent"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Experimental"}]]}]}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"Working exploration, not a product"}]]}],["$","div","concept",{"className":"flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"shrink-0","children":["$","span",null,{"title":"Designed, not yet built","className":"label-mono inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"inline-block h-[6px] w-[6px] rounded-full bg-muted-foreground/50"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Concept"}]]}]}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"Designed, not yet built"}]]}],["$","div","ongoing",{"className":"flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"shrink-0","children":["$","span",null,{"title":"Continuous practice, not a deliverable","className":"label-mono inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"inline-block h-[6px] w-[6px] rounded-full bg-foreground"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Ongoing"}]]}]}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"Continuous practice, not a deliverable"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","$Lf",null,{"projects":[{"slug":"amazon-express-logistic","name":"Amazon Express Logistic","tagline":"FOR TARCKING PACKAGES ","summary":"FOR TARCKING PACKAGES ","category":"software","status":"active","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"Amazon-Express-Logistic","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["TypeScript"],"cover":"terminal","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["A modern courier delivery and tracking system built with **React.js** and **Supabase**.","It allows users to send packages, generate tracking IDs, and track deliveries in real-time.","- Send package delivery requests"],"problem":"Addressing challenges in software with reliable, modern tooling.","intendedUsers":"Developers, engineers, and users seeking reliable tooling.","keyFeatures":["Modular architecture","Documented repository"],"implementation":["Primary language: TypeScript","Repository features 0 stars and continuous maintenance."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Ensuring robustness and performance.","solution":"Applied clean code practices and modular structure."}],"status":"Active and maintained.","future":["Expand feature set","Enhance performance and documentation"]}},{"slug":"xl-driv","name":"XL DRIV","tagline":"XLDriv is Nigeria's mobility platform where riders negotiate their own fares for city trips, interstate travel, and courier delivery, while drivers keep 100% of their earnings with 0% commission on a ₦2,450 flat daily subscription.","summary":"XLDriv is Nigeria's mobility platform where riders negotiate their own fares for city trips, interstate travel, and courier delivery, while drivers keep 100% of their earnings with 0% commission on a ₦2,450 flat daily subscription.","category":"ai","status":"active","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"XL-DRIV","liveUrl":"xldriv.com","tech":["TypeScript"],"cover":"terminal","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Set your fare, negotiate with drivers, and keep 100% of your earnings.","**XLDriv** is a next-generation Nigerian mobility and logistics web platform engineered to break away from traditional high-commission aggregator models. Built with a people-first ethos and modern web standards, XLDriv empowers passengers to propose and negotiate their own fares, while offering driver-partners an industry-disrupting **0% commission** model powered by a flat daily subscription of **₦2,450**.","Beyond urban ride-hailing, XLDriv anchors a full-spectrum multimodal transport network spanning **City Rides**, **City-to-City (Interstate) trips**, **Ride-Sharing**, **Express Courier Deliveries**, and an industrial **Trucking & Port Customs Clearing** division based in Lagos."],"problem":"Addressing challenges in ai with reliable, modern tooling.","intendedUsers":"Developers, engineers, and users seeking reliable tooling.","keyFeatures":["Modular architecture","Documented repository"],"implementation":["Primary language: TypeScript","Repository features 1 stars and continuous maintenance."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Ensuring robustness and performance.","solution":"Applied clean code practices and modular structure."}],"status":"Active and maintained.","future":["Expand feature set","Enhance performance and documentation"]}},{"slug":"tinubulux","name":"TINUBULUX","tagline":"A Tinubu-themed DDOS tool .....designed to stress anything 😃 ","summary":"A Tinubu-themed DDOS tool .....designed to stress anything 😃 ","category":"software","status":"active","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"TINUBULUX","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":[],"cover":"window","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["<img src=\"file_00000000aa7071f4a447333952d6ec10.png\" alt=\"TINUBULUX Banner\" width=\"800\"/>","> *\"Real power. No simulations. Built by Anonymous-beta.\"*","High-performance network stress testing framework. Multi-vector. Multi-process. Terminal UI. Linux-native."],"problem":"Addressing challenges in software with reliable, modern tooling.","intendedUsers":"Developers, engineers, and users seeking reliable tooling.","keyFeatures":["Modular architecture","Documented repository"],"implementation":["Primary language: TypeScript / Python","Repository features 0 stars and continuous maintenance."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Ensuring robustness and performance.","solution":"Applied clean code practices and modular structure."}],"status":"Active and maintained.","future":["Expand feature set","Enhance performance and documentation"]}},{"slug":"taiwoade-hub","name":"Taiwo Adebola Profile Hub","tagline":"GitHub workspace, central identity and open-source project catalogue.","summary":"Central GitHub profile repository documenting engineering projects, cybersecurity studies, and self-hosted infrastructure.","category":"ai","status":"active","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"taiwoade-hub","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Markdown","GitHub Actions","Shell"],"cover":"terminal","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Special profile workspace and repository showcase for Taiwo Adebola on GitHub.","Acts as the central activity hub detailing projects across cybersecurity, software development, DevOps infrastructure, and automation."],"problem":"Providing a verified, unified profile hub for engineering work and experiments.","intendedUsers":"Collaborators, recruiters, and open-source developers.","keyFeatures":["Unified activity board","Curated repository directory","System & tooling credentials"],"implementation":["Engineered with GitHub profile conventions and automation workflows.","Integrated with live repository statistics."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Keeping metrics and project statuses current.","solution":"Standardized links and honest project labelling."}],"status":"Active and maintained.","future":["Continuous additions of new engineering tools and case studies"]}},{"slug":"order-of-service","name":"Order of Service","tagline":"Real-time service planning for church teams.","summary":"A modern church order-of-service planning application with real-time document preview, inline editing and reusable liturgy blueprints — built so teams stop juggling Word files and WhatsApp threads.","category":"software","status":"in-development","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"order-of-service","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Next.js","TypeScript","React","Tailwind CSS"],"cover":"window","featured":true,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Order of Service is a planning application for drafting, refining and sharing the running order of church services. Instead of a Word document attached to a group chat, the service programme lives in a structured, editable document that updates its preview as you type.","The project began as a fix for a workflow I saw first-hand: service outlines passed around as files, revised out-of-order, and handed to the media team too late to be useful. It has grown into a broader exploration of document-style applications — how structure, templates and live previews change the way teams plan a recurring event."],"problem":"Service programmes are recurring, structural documents that are treated like one-off files. Every week the same sections are re-typed, changes arrive via chat messages, and the people who present the service — media, musicians, coordinators — each see a different version of the truth. The problem is not document editing; it is keeping a structured document alive across a team without a specialised tool.","intendedUsers":"Church service coordinators who draft the programme, administrators who maintain liturgy content, and media/production teams who need the final running order accurately and early enough to rehearse.","keyFeatures":["Real-time preview of the running order as it is edited","Inline editing of individual items directly in the flow of the document","Liturgy blueprints — reusable templates for recurring service patterns","Typed sections (songs, scripture, announcements) instead of free-form text","A clean, print-friendly output for sharing the final programme"],"implementation":["The application models a service as a typed document: an ordered collection of section blocks, each with its own fields and presentation rules. That model is what makes everything else possible — the preview, the blueprint templates and the print output are all projections of the same structured data rather than separate artefacts.","The editing experience is built around optimistic, inline updates: selecting an item focuses it in context, and the preview pane reflects the change immediately. Blueprints are simply frozen documents whose sections can be cloned into a new draft, which keeps templating cheap to implement and easy to reason about."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"A service document has to be two things at once: an editor (dense, structured, keyboard-friendly) and a programme (clean, readable, presentable). Naively these pull in opposite directions.","solution":"Treating them as two views over one data model. The editor works on typed blocks; the preview renders the same blocks with presentation styles. No syncing layer is needed because there is only ever one source of truth."},{"challenge":"Liturgy varies widely between churches — section types that fit one tradition are noise to another.","solution":"Starting from a small, opinionated set of section types and keeping the block schema open, so new section kinds can be added without reworking the document model."}],"status":"In active development. The document model, inline editing and preview flow are the current focus; blueprint management follows. Statuses on this page are maintained by hand.","future":["Collaborative editing for coordinators and media teams","A presentation mode for projection during the service itself","Cloud sync with offline-friendly drafts","A mobile companion for last-minute changes backstage"]}},{"slug":"xl-drive","name":"XL Drive","tagline":"A ride-hailing web experience with a clear booking journey.","summary":"A ride-hailing website project inspired by modern transportation platforms — a responsive interface with polished interactions and booking-oriented user journeys, built to study product-grade front-end work.","category":"software","status":"in-development","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"xl-drive","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["React","TypeScript","Tailwind CSS"],"cover":"map","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["XL Drive is a front-end study of what a modern ride-hailing product feels like on the web. It focuses on the part riders actually experience: choosing a route, selecting a vehicle class, understanding a fare and confirming a trip — in a responsive interface that stays fast on modest devices and connections.","The goal of the project is craft. Ride-hailing is a saturated category with mature conventions, which makes it an ideal training ground for interaction design: there is a well-understood user journey to measure my work against, and nothing to hide behind."],"problem":"Multi-step booking flows are where front-end quality is most visible — and easiest to get wrong. State has to stay coherent across steps, loading and empty states matter, and the interface must remain usable on a small screen in one hand. The project exists to practise that whole surface area against a realistic domain.","intendedUsers":"Riders on mobile web — the hardest case — plus anyone evaluating the front-end as a portfolio piece.","keyFeatures":["A step-by-step booking flow with a clear sense of progress","Responsive layouts designed mobile-first","Vehicle class and fare presentation with honest loading states","Polished micro-interactions that never block the flow"],"implementation":["The interface is componentised around the booking state machine: each step renders from the current state, and transitions animate opacity and transform only, keeping the flow smooth without layout thrash.","Styling is utility-first with a small set of design tokens, so spacing, radius and elevation stay consistent across the many small components a booking UI produces."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Making a multi-step flow feel light rather than like a long questionnaire.","solution":"Reducing each step to one decision, communicating progress without clutter, and allowing back-navigation without losing entered state."}],"status":"In development as a front-end project. No backend booking service is claimed — that is explicitly future work.","future":["Interactive map integration for pickup and destination selection","A driver-side view to explore the other half of the marketplace","A real booking API behind the interface"]}},{"slug":"terrific-bank","name":"Terrific Bank · Terry Coin","tagline":"A fintech prototype: wallets, transfers and multi-currency ledgers.","summary":"A financial-technology prototype exploring digital wallets, currency conversion, transfers and ledger records, with Terry Coin as its native currency concept — a study of how money movement should actually be modelled in software.","category":"software","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"terrific-bank","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["TypeScript","React","SQL"],"cover":"ledger","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Terrific Bank is a prototype for a digital wallet application: accounts in multiple currencies, transfers between them, conversion, and a transaction ledger that records every movement. Terry Coin is the native currency concept woven through it — the fictional asset that gives the prototype its identity.","The project is really about ledgers. Balances are easy to render; the interesting engineering question is how to record money movement so that history is complete, explainable and auditable — the difference between a number that happens to be displayed and a system that knows what happened."],"problem":"Payment apps look simple from the outside and are quietly rigorous underneath. Building a credible prototype means confronting that rigour: every transfer touches two sides, conversion happens at a rate that must be recorded, and the ledger has to stay consistent even when the interface makes change look instant.","intendedUsers":"Prototype users moving and converting currency — and me, as the engineer learning the domain by constructing it.","keyFeatures":["Multi-currency wallets with per-currency balances","Transfers between accounts with complete ledger records","Currency conversion with recorded exchange rates","A transaction history that explains every movement","Terry Coin as a native currency concept within the wallet"],"implementation":["The design direction is ledger-first: money movement is recorded as immutable ledger entries, and balances are derived views over those entries rather than the source of truth. This mirrors how serious financial systems think and turns correctness into a property of the data model instead of the UI code.","Conversion is modelled as two-sided movement — value leaves one currency and arrives in another at a recorded rate — so the ledger reads as a coherent story rather than an unexplained balance jump."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Conversion is easy to present and easy to get subtly wrong — rounding, ordering, and the temptation to mutate balances directly.","solution":"Recording every conversion as paired ledger entries with the applied rate, and treating displayed balances as derived state. It is more work up front and dramatically easier to reason about afterwards."}],"status":"Concept / prototype stage. It is a domain study, not a financial service — no real funds, no production claims.","future":["Full double-entry structure with strict invariants","Statements and exportable history","Budgeting views over the ledger"]}},{"slug":"productivityos","name":"ProductivityOS","tagline":"A distraction-free workspace built around a timed Focus Mode.","summary":"A productivity application concept centred on a timed Focus Mode and a calm, distraction-free workspace — enough structure to organise deep work, without becoming another thing to manage.","category":"software","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["TypeScript","React"],"cover":"grid","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["ProductivityOS is a concept for a workspace that takes focus seriously. Its centrepiece is a timed Focus Mode: a single, deliberate block of time in which the interface contracts to the work at hand and everything else recedes.","The concept is a reaction to productivity tools that multiply surfaces — boards, dashboards, charts — until managing the system replaces doing the work. The design question here is the opposite: what is the minimum structure that supports deep work?"],"problem":"Most productivity software optimises for organising intentions rather than protecting attention. The problem this concept explores is an interface that is structurally committed to a single focus session at a time.","intendedUsers":"Knowledge workers and students who want a workspace that helps them start, hold and finish a focused block of work.","keyFeatures":["A timed Focus Mode with a deliberately reduced interface","A distraction-free workspace surface for the session's work","Session history to see focus accumulate over time","Lightweight organisation of what is up next"],"implementation":["As a concept, the emphasis so far is interaction design: how the interface contracts when a session starts, how the timer stays present without becoming pressure, and how the workspace restores afterwards. The implementation approach follows the design — a session state machine that the whole UI subscribes to."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Timers are trivial; making time visible without making it anxious is not.","solution":"Designing the timer as ambient state — perceptible at a glance, never flashing, never counting down loudly — and letting the user choose how present it is."}],"status":"Concept. Interaction direction is being explored before deeper implementation.","future":["Ambient session soundscapes and end-of-session rituals","Integration with the Todo, Habits and Goals system"]}},{"slug":"todo-habits-goals","name":"Todo, Habits & Goals","tagline":"Tasks, habits and goals in one connected system.","summary":"A personal-productivity concept connecting task management, habit streaks, goal breakdown, calendar integration and Telegram notifications — the connected system I wanted for myself and couldn't find shaped quite this way.","category":"software","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["TypeScript","SQL","Telegram Bot API"],"cover":"grid","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["This project connects three things that usually live in three apps: what I need to do (tasks), what I want to keep doing (habits), and where I am heading (goals). The concept links them — goals decompose into tasks, tasks repeat as habits, and the calendar shows the whole shape of a week.","The distinctive piece is delivery: reminders and digests arrive through Telegram, which is already where attention goes on a phone. A productivity system that lives behind an app icon competes with every other app; one that arrives as a message meets you where you are."],"problem":"Fragmentation. Tasks, habits and goals drift apart because they are stored apart, and reminders fail when they require opening an app. The concept treats the three as one connected model with one delivery channel.","intendedUsers":"Individuals — starting with me — who want a single honest view of commitments, routines and direction.","keyFeatures":["Task management with scheduling and priorities","Habit tracking with streaks and history","Goal breakdown into tasks and milestones","A calendar view that unifies all three","Telegram notifications for reminders and daily digests"],"implementation":["The data model joins the three domains through shared scheduling: a calendar entry can originate from a task, a habit or a goal milestone, which keeps one timeline truthful. The Telegram side is a bot that reads the schedule and pushes reminders — delivery logic lives outside the core model so other channels can be added without touching it."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Connected systems breed notification noise — everything wants to remind you of itself.","solution":"A single daily digest as the default, with per-item opt-in for immediate reminders. Quiet by default, loud only on request."}],"status":"Concept. The Telegram notification path is the piece with the most prior hands-on experience behind it.","future":["Natural-language quick capture through the bot","Weekly review flows over goals and habits"]}},{"slug":"developer-portfolio","name":"Developer Portfolio","tagline":"This website — an exercise in restraint.","summary":"My personal engineering portfolio: Next.js App Router, TypeScript and Tailwind CSS with carefully budgeted motion, a maintained project catalogue, a GitHub integration with graceful fallback, and a real contact pipeline.","category":"software","status":"active","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"/","tech":["Next.js","TypeScript","Tailwind CSS","Motion","Prisma"],"cover":"window","featured":true,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["The portfolio you are reading is itself a project: a deliberately restrained, typography-first website that presents my work without pretending to be something it is not. Every section — including this case study — is generated from a structured project catalogue rather than hand-built pages.","The interesting constraints were self-imposed: honest content only (no invented metrics or screenshots), Server Components by default, animation only where it earns its keep, and a GitHub integration that degrades gracefully instead of breaking when the API is unavailable."],"problem":"A portfolio has three audiences — recruiters scanning for signal, clients evaluating capability, and engineers judging craft — and it must serve all three without bloat. The deeper problem: it is easy for a portfolio to imply more than its owner can defend. This one is built to be defensible.","intendedUsers":"Recruiters, potential clients and fellow developers who want to understand what I build and how I think.","keyFeatures":["A structured project catalogue driving cards, filters and every case-study page","GitHub integration with cached API requests and a local fallback catalogue","A real contact form — validated, spam-protected, stored in a database","Light and dark themes with no flash and full reduced-motion support","A downloadable CV and honest project status labels","Semantic HTML, keyboard-friendly navigation, sitemap and Open Graph metadata"],"implementation":["Pages are Server Components by default; client components exist only where interactivity demands them — navigation state, theme toggle, project filters, animations and the contact form. Motion is confined to opacity and transforms, driven by viewport detection, and disabled for users who prefer reduced motion.","The GitHub integration reads the configured username server-side, caches responses, and falls back to the local catalogue on failure or rate limits — the page never breaks because an API did. All professional links are environment-driven and simply do not render when unconfigured, which is how the site avoids inventing URLs."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Modelling honesty in data. 'Concept', 'in development' and 'active' mean very different things, and most portfolio templates flatten them all into 'project'.","solution":"A status field with explicit labels and plain-language notes on every case study — the catalogue is the single place statuses are maintained, and every page inherits them."},{"challenge":"Keeping client-side JavaScript small while still feeling alive.","solution":"Doing structure and content in Server Components, delegating simple hover and active feedback to CSS transitions, and reserving motion for entrances and state changes that genuinely benefit from it."}],"status":"Live and maintained. It evolves with the project catalogue — which is the point of building it this way.","future":["MDX-based authoring for case studies and articles","Full-text search across projects and writing","Live GitHub activity when the profile is configured"]}},{"slug":"verify-hub","name":"Verify Hub","tagline":"Website verification and inspection, reported where you already work.","summary":"A website verification and inspection project exploring URL checks, browser automation, screenshots, error reporting, GitHub integration and Telegram notifications — so checks land where the team already is.","category":"security","status":"in-development","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"verify-hub","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","Browser Automation","Telegram Bot API","GitHub API"],"cover":"rings","featured":true,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Verify Hub is a verification pipeline for websites: give it a URL and it checks availability, drives a real browser to capture what the page actually rendered, collects errors, and reports the result through Telegram and GitHub — the channels a small team already watches.","It sits at the intersection of my interests in browser automation and web reliability, and it is scoped deliberately: it verifies availability, rendering and configuration. It does not claim to be a comprehensive security scanner, and its reports are careful to say so."],"problem":"Knowing whether a deployment actually works is scattered across curl, DevTools, uptime dashboards and memory. For a small team — or one developer wearing every hat — the checks exist but the reporting never arrives where decisions happen. The problem is delivery as much as detection.","intendedUsers":"Developers and webmasters who want scheduled or on-demand verification of their deployments, reported into chat and issue trackers rather than another dashboard.","keyFeatures":["URL availability and response checks","Browser-automated page rendering with screenshot capture","Error collection from the page and console","GitHub integration for filing and tracking what broke","Telegram notifications for immediate reporting"],"implementation":["The architecture is a pipeline: check → capture → evaluate → report. Each stage is independent, which keeps the core honest — the check stage can run without reporting, and reporting can be extended without touching capture. Provider-based notification means Telegram and GitHub are implementations of one notifier interface, not hardwired paths.","Browser automation runs real page loads with explicit timeouts and retries, because flaky automation that cries wolf destroys trust in the reports. Captures are treated as evidence attached to a verdict, not as the verdict itself."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Browser automation is inherently flaky — slow pages, transient DNS, headless environment quirks. A verification tool that fails randomly is worse than no tool.","solution":"Explicit timeout budgets per stage, retries with backoff for transient failures, and distinguishing 'the site is broken' from 'the check could not complete' in every report."},{"challenge":"The temptation to over-claim: availability checks drifting into implying security assurance.","solution":"Scoping the language. Reports describe what was checked, and the documentation is explicit that this is availability and rendering verification — not a security audit."}],"status":"In development. The check-and-capture pipeline is the current focus; notification integrations are being wired behind the provider interface.","future":["Scheduled recurring checks with change detection","TLS certificate and response-header checks","Visual diffs between captures","A small web dashboard over the history"]}},{"slug":"dataguard","name":"DataGuard","tagline":"Making data visible, organised and monitored.","summary":"A data-management and security-oriented dashboard concept built on SQLite — exploring what it means to give data the same visibility, organisation and monitoring care that we give to interfaces.","category":"security","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["SQL","SQLite","TypeScript"],"cover":"rings","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["DataGuard is a dashboard concept for working with structured data deliberately: seeing what exists, organising it, and monitoring how it changes. It is built on SQLite, which keeps the whole stack local and inspectable — the database file is the system, not a black box behind a service.","The security orientation is about posture rather than scanning: knowing what data you hold, where it lives, and what changed is the foundation every other security practice builds on. The concept treats that visibility as a first-class interface problem."],"problem":"Data accumulates silently. Tables grow, duplicates drift, and nobody can say precisely what is stored or what changed last month. Security thinking starts with inventory — and inventory is exactly what most small projects lack.","intendedUsers":"Developers and small teams who want a real picture of the data their applications actually hold.","keyFeatures":["Organised, browsable views over structured data","Visibility layers — what exists, where, and how much","Monitoring views over data growth and change","A local-first SQLite foundation with no external dependencies"],"implementation":["The concept leans on SQLite's strengths: a single-file, zero-configuration database that can be inspected with standard tooling. The dashboard is a lens over schema and content rather than an ownership layer — it shows what is there instead of hiding it."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Dashboards drift into decoration — charts that look like monitoring but convey nothing actionable.","solution":"Starting from questions ('what changed?', 'what grew?', 'what is unused?') and letting views earn their place by answering them."}],"status":"Concept. Schema exploration and view design are the current work.","future":["Change-log views with diffs","Data retention and cleanup suggestions","Export and reporting"]}},{"slug":"web-intelligence-platform","name":"Web Intelligence Platform","tagline":"Examining what websites reveal about themselves.","summary":"A website-inspection and investigation concept for examining technologies, page structure, publicly accessible information, configuration issues and potential vulnerabilities — a workbench for learning how the web leaks information.","category":"security","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","Browser Automation","HTTP Analysis"],"cover":"nodes","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["The Web Intelligence Platform is a concept for an investigation workbench: point it at a website and it assembles a picture of what that site exposes — technologies in use, page structure, publicly accessible files and endpoints, configuration observations, and potential vulnerabilities flagged for human review.","It is, openly, a learning instrument. Web security is learned by looking at real surfaces and asking what they reveal, and the platform is the structured way to do that looking — the tool I want to exist while I study."],"problem":"Security assessment output is only as good as its honesty. Automated tools either drown the user in false certainty or hide their methodology. The problem this concept attacks is traceability: every observation should show the evidence it came from, and every finding should be a lead, not a verdict.","intendedUsers":"Security learners and analysts examining sites they are authorised to assess, who want observations they can verify rather than a score they can't.","keyFeatures":["Technology fingerprinting from page and header evidence","Page structure and metadata mapping","Discovery of publicly exposed files and endpoints","Configuration observations — headers, cookies, redirects","Potential-vulnerability flags framed explicitly as leads for manual review"],"implementation":["Findings are modelled as evidence chains: a claim, the raw observation behind it, and a confidence framing. This structure is what keeps the tool honest — nothing is reported that cannot show its source, and the 'potential vulnerability' label is reserved for observations a human must confirm.","Collection uses standard HTTP analysis plus browser automation for what only a rendered page reveals, with the two kept clearly separated because their evidentiary weight differs."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"The line between investigation and intrusion is thin, and tools in this space are easily misused.","solution":"Designing for authorised targets, deliberate scope, and report language that never overstates — plus an approach that treats findings as study material first. Ethics are part of the design, not a disclaimer bolted on."}],"status":"Concept — architecture and evidence model being designed.","future":["Report export with full evidence trails","Comparison between scans over time","A study mode that explains why each finding matters"]}},{"slug":"miro","name":"Miro · Miro Alex","tagline":"A Telegram AI and music bot with memory.","summary":"A Telegram bot built with Python and aiogram blending conversational AI, music features, per-user memory and administrative commands — my longest-running experiment in making a bot feel alive rather than mechanical.","category":"ai","status":"experimental","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"miro","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","aiogram","LLM APIs","SQLite"],"cover":"nodes","featured":true,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Miro is a Telegram bot with a personality. Around a Python and aiogram core, it combines conversational AI, music-related features, memory of the people it talks to, and the administrative commands a group actually needs. Its sibling variant, Miro Alex, carries the same architecture with a different character.","The project is where I learned that the hard part of a bot is not replying — it is everything around replying: holding state across conversations, surviving restarts, respecting rate limits, and behaving consistently when a dozen people are talking at once."],"problem":"Most bots are vending machines: press a command, receive a canned response. The problem Miro explores is continuity — a bot that remembers who you are, participates in the ambient conversation of a group, and still performs concrete jobs like music playback and admin tasks when asked.","intendedUsers":"Telegram group members who want a bot that is useful and pleasant to have around, and group admins who need moderation tooling that blends in.","keyFeatures":["Conversational AI woven into ordinary group chat","Music features and playback-related commands","Memory integration — the bot retains context about users over time","Administrative commands for group management","A consistent personality across features"],"implementation":["The aiogram layer is organised as domain handlers — conversation, music, memory, admin — behind a shared context object, so features compose instead of colliding. Memory lives in SQLite keyed per user and per chat, which keeps recall cheap and restart-safe.","AI responses are bounded and purposeful: the bot knows when it is being addressed, keeps replies within a deliberate length, and falls back gracefully when the AI backend is slow or unavailable. The bot is a long-running process, so every subsystem is written to fail independently rather than take the process down."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"A long-running bot is a reliability problem wearing a chat interface. Memory leaks, unawaited tasks and unhandled exceptions all surface as the bot going quiet — the worst failure mode, because it looks like absence.","solution":"Strict lifecycle discipline around the event loop, supervisor-style restarts, error boundaries per handler, and logging that makes 'why did Miro stop talking' answerable from logs alone."},{"challenge":"Memory cuts both ways: remembering everything is creepy and expensive; remembering nothing defeats the point.","solution":"Scoped, purposeful memory — the bot keeps what serves the conversation (names, preferences, running jokes) and expires what doesn't, with retention rules per memory type."}],"status":"Experimental — actively iterated on and used in the small groups it serves. It is a hobby-scale system and is described that way.","future":["A plugin architecture so features can be enabled per chat","Smarter memory retrieval instead of full-context injection","Voice interaction in chats"]}},{"slug":"jurix","name":"Jurix","tagline":"A Telegram bot project.","summary":"A Telegram bot project. I'm deliberately keeping this description minimal until I revisit the repository and document what it actually does — a case study written from memory would be a case study with invented details.","category":"ai","status":"experimental","statusNote":"Functionality to be confirmed from the repository","repo":"jurix","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Telegram Bot API"],"cover":"nodes","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Jurix is a Telegram bot project from my automation work. That is, honestly, everything this page should claim today: the full case study will be written after a proper repository review, with features documented from the code rather than from memory.","I hold my project pages to a simple standard — nothing is described as working unless the source confirms it. This page is what that standard looks like when applied to itself."],"problem":"To be documented during the repository review, alongside the feature inventory and architecture notes.","intendedUsers":"To be confirmed from the repository.","keyFeatures":["Telegram bot functionality — details pending repository review"],"implementation":["Pending review. The architecture section will cover how the bot is structured once the source has been re-read."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Documenting a project accurately months after touching it last.","solution":"Not documenting it from memory at all — reviewing the repository first, then writing. This page will be replaced by the real case study."}],"status":"Experimental. A repository review is scheduled to determine the accurate current state and feature set.","future":["A complete, code-verified case study on this page"]}},{"slug":"alphahunter","name":"AlphaHunter","tagline":"An AI agent concept for the Mantle and Somnia ecosystems.","summary":"An AI-agent concept exploring the Mantle and Somnia ecosystems — what an autonomous assistant could meaningfully observe and do on-chain, built as an investigation rather than a product.","category":"ai","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","LLM APIs"],"cover":"nodes","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["AlphaHunter is a concept for an AI agent that lives in the Mantle and Somnia ecosystems. The interest is not trading automation — it is agency: what can an LLM-driven agent perceive on-chain, what actions are meaningful, and where does autonomy stop being useful and start being risk.","The project treats the agent loop itself as the product: perception of ecosystem state, reasoning over what changed, and a constrained set of actions with human-visible explanations for every decision."],"problem":"Agent frameworks are easy to demo and hard to trust. The concept explores the boring-but-decisive parts: bounded action spaces, explainable decisions, and failure modes that degrade to 'ask the human' instead of improvising.","intendedUsers":"Developers and explorers of on-chain ecosystems who want an assistant that surfaces what matters instead of acting on its own initiative.","keyFeatures":["Ecosystem observation across Mantle and Somnia","An agent loop with explicitly constrained actions","Explainable decisions — every action carries its reasoning","Human-in-the-loop defaults for anything consequential"],"implementation":["The concept architecture separates perception (data collection from the ecosystems), cognition (LLM reasoning over observations) and action (a whitelist of operations the agent may perform). The action whitelist is the safety model — autonomy is earned per action, not assumed."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"On-chain actions are irreversible, which makes agent errors expensive in a way web-app errors never are.","solution":"Defaulting to observation and recommendation; any execution path is opt-in, bounded and simulated before it is real."}],"status":"Concept. Ecosystem research and agent architecture exploration.","future":["A simulation environment for agent behaviour","Action audit logs with human-readable reasoning"]}},{"slug":"desktop-voice-ai","name":"Desktop Voice AI","tagline":"An offline-first Ubuntu desktop assistant.","summary":"A desktop assistant concept for Ubuntu focused on voice interaction, offline speech recognition and desktop integration — AI that works on your machine without depending on someone else's cloud.","category":"ai","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","Speech Recognition","Linux Desktop APIs"],"cover":"rings","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Desktop Voice AI is a concept for an Ubuntu assistant you talk to. Voice in, actions out: launch applications, find files, control the desktop — with speech recognition that runs locally and keeps working when the network doesn't.","The project is interesting to me precisely because of its constraints. Offline recognition trades accuracy for autonomy and privacy, and Linux desktop integration is unfashionable, under-documented territory — which makes it a genuinely good place to learn how systems fit together."],"problem":"Voice assistants are overwhelmingly cloud products: convenient, always-listening, and useless without connectivity. The concept asks what a credible local-first alternative looks like on the desktop most people don't associate with voice at all.","intendedUsers":"Linux users who want hands-free control of their machine without sending their voice to a third party.","keyFeatures":["Voice input with offline speech recognition","Desktop actions — launching apps, finding files, system queries","A local-first design that degrades gracefully offline","Responses that respect the terminal-first nature of Linux"],"implementation":["The concept pipeline is: capture → local speech-to-text → intent mapping → desktop action. Intent mapping is deliberately rule-based rather than LLM-dependent for core actions, so the assistant's basics survive without any AI backend; language model integration is an enhancement layer, not a dependency."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Offline speech recognition quality varies dramatically across accents and hardware.","solution":"Designing commands as short, structured phrases the recognizer can be tuned for, rather than free-form conversation — matching the assistant's scope to what local models actually do well."}],"status":"Concept. Feasibility work on recognition quality and desktop APIs.","future":["Wake-word detection","A small plugin system for custom actions"]}},{"slug":"linklens","name":"LinkLens","tagline":"A command-line media utility, built around yt-dlp.","summary":"A CLI media utility that wraps yt-dlp with a friendlier workflow — inspecting links, listing formats and handling batches — with a graphical interface planned once the core settles.","category":"ai","status":"in-development","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"linklens","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","yt-dlp","CLI"],"cover":"pipeline","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["LinkLens is a command-line utility for working with media links. Built on yt-dlp, it focuses on the workflow around downloading: inspecting what a link actually contains, comparing formats and quality, and processing many links at once without babysitting each one.","The name is the intent — a lens on links. Rather than memorising extractor flags, the tool answers the questions you actually have before downloading: what is this, what quality exists, and what will I get."],"problem":"yt-dlp is extraordinarily capable and correspondingly dense. For everyday tasks — check a link, pick a sensible quality, grab a batch — the interface tax is real. The problem LinkLens solves is ergonomics: a small, predictable surface over a large, capable engine.","intendedUsers":"People who live in a terminal and want media handling to be a one-liner, not a research project.","keyFeatures":["Link inspection with available formats and metadata","Quality and format selection with sensible defaults","Batch processing of many links","A clean CLI with helpful errors","A graphical interface, planned"],"implementation":["LinkLens wraps yt-dlp as an engine rather than reimplementing extraction — metadata queries flow through it, results are normalised into a small set of meaningful options, and the CLI layer owns all the ergonomics. Keeping the engine swappable is deliberate: upstream does extraction best, and the value here is the interface."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Wrapping a fast-moving upstream tool: extractors break, options change.","solution":"A thin integration layer with the CLI owning defaults, plus honest error messages when an extractor fails rather than pretending the link is bad."}],"status":"In development as a CLI. The graphical interface is planned, not started — and labelled accordingly.","future":["The planned graphical interface","Output presets for common destinations","Library-style mode for use from other tools"]}},{"slug":"runx","name":"RunX","tagline":"A Visual Studio Code extension project.","summary":"A Visual Studio Code extension project. Like Jurix, this page stays deliberately minimal until I complete a repository review — the feature list will come from the code, not from optimism.","category":"ai","status":"experimental","statusNote":"Features to be confirmed from the repository","repo":"runx","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["TypeScript","VS Code Extension API"],"cover":"grid","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["RunX is a Visual Studio Code extension project — an exploration of developer tooling inside the editor, where the smallest friction reduction pays off many times a day.","What it does today is a question I will answer from the source: the honest version of this page arrives after a repository review documents the extension's actual commands, configuration and behaviour. Until then, this is intentionally a placeholder that admits it."],"problem":"To be documented during the repository review — what friction the extension removes and for whom.","intendedUsers":"To be confirmed from the repository.","keyFeatures":["VS Code extension functionality — details pending repository review"],"implementation":["Pending review; the extension architecture and command surface will be documented from the code."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Extensions rot quietly — APIs deprecate, the marketplace moves on, and 'it worked when I shipped it' is not documentation.","solution":"The planned review doubles as maintenance: verify against current VS Code APIs, then write the case study."}],"status":"Experimental. Feature documentation is pending a repository review.","future":["A complete, code-verified case study on this page","Publication to the marketplace if the review justifies it"]}},{"slug":"location-mapper","name":"Location Mapper","tagline":"Interactive maps with open-source tooling.","summary":"An interactive mapping project exploring open-source mapping libraries — global navigation, location visualisation and what maps can communicate that lists never can.","category":"ai","status":"experimental","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["TypeScript","OpenStreetMap","Mapping Libraries"],"cover":"map","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Location Mapper is an exploration of interactive maps as an interface medium. Using open-source mapping libraries and open map data, it covers the fundamentals of geographic visualisation: plotting locations, navigating globally, and rendering data in a spatial context.","Maps are a distinct engineering discipline from typical UI work — tile rendering, projection, zoom-dependent detail, and interaction patterns that feel wrong if ported naively from non-spatial interfaces. The project exists to learn that discipline properly."],"problem":"Location data is intuitive when mapped and opaque when listed. The problem is the gap between 'a table of coordinates' and 'an interface where space is legible' — projection, performance at different zooms, and interaction that respects how people actually read maps.","intendedUsers":"Anyone exploring what spatial presentation does for a dataset — the project itself being the current audience as much as any end user.","keyFeatures":["Interactive mapping with global navigation","Location visualisation over open map data","Zoom-aware presentation of detail","Open-source libraries throughout — no proprietary map dependencies"],"implementation":["Built on open-source mapping libraries over open data (OpenStreetMap ecosystem), keeping every layer inspectable. The interesting work is in the data layer: converting ordinary lists into map-ready collections with sensible clustering and zoom behaviour."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Performance degrades non-linearly with the number of rendered points, and the naive fix — always clustering — throws away the detail maps exist to show.","solution":"Zoom-dependent strategies: aggregate at low zoom, reveal detail as the viewport narrows, so density and detail trade off deliberately instead of accidentally."}],"status":"Experimental — a working exploration rather than a product.","future":["Richer data overlays and heat visualisation","Route drawing and distance tooling"]}},{"slug":"terminal-chat","name":"Terminal Chat","tagline":"Chat where developers live: the terminal.","summary":"A terminal-based chat application concept — exploring what communication looks like when the interface is text, and what a TUI can do that a GUI quietly can't.","category":"ai","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Terminal UI","WebSockets"],"cover":"terminal","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Terminal Chat is a concept for conversation without leaving the terminal. The premise is the developer's reality: the shell is already home, and a chat that lives there — keyboard-first, scriptable, composable with everything else — is a different product from a smaller web chat, not a lesser one.","The interesting design space is what TUIs do uniquely well: density, speed, keyboard-native interaction, and integration with the pipeline idioms of the command line."],"problem":"Chat applications pull context-switches: leave the terminal, enter a window, return. The concept asks whether lightweight, terminal-resident communication is viable for the cases that matter — quick questions, standing rooms, pair-debugging conversations.","intendedUsers":"Developers and operators who keep a terminal open the way others keep a browser open.","keyFeatures":["Terminal-native chat interface","Keyboard-first interaction throughout","Multiple channels or conversations from one session","Real-time messaging over WebSockets (planned transport)"],"implementation":["As a concept, the architecture is deliberately minimal: a TUI client speaking to a relay service over WebSockets, with presence and history as the two server-side responsibilities. The TUI layer is the design centre — layout, scrollback and keyboard interaction decide whether the concept is credible."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Terminal rendering varies wildly across emulators, fonts and platforms — a TUI that is beautiful in one is broken in another.","solution":"Targeting a conservative feature set of the TUI vocabulary and testing across the emulators that matter, rather than chasing pixel-perfect terminal art."}],"status":"Concept.","future":["A working relay and TUI prototype","Integration with existing chat transports"]}},{"slug":"coolify-deployment","name":"Coolify Deployment Infrastructure","tagline":"My practical self-hosting workbench.","summary":"Documented hands-on work deploying applications with Coolify — connecting GitHub repositories, configuring domains, troubleshooting DNS and keeping services running on my own infrastructure.","category":"devops","status":"ongoing","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Coolify","Docker","Ubuntu","DNS","GitHub"],"cover":"pipeline","featured":true,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["This entry documents practice, not a software artefact: my ongoing work standing up and operating a self-hosted deployment environment on Coolify. Applications are deployed from GitHub repositories, exposed on configured domains, and kept running through real troubleshooting — the unglamorous half of engineering that tutorials skip.","It is featured in my portfolio deliberately. Deployment is where software stops being a repository and starts being a system, and doing it yourself — on hardware you control, with a reverse proxy you configured — teaches the full request path in a way no managed platform ever will."],"problem":"I wanted deployments I could understand end-to-end: from git push, through build, to a running container behind a reverse proxy with a domain pointed at it. Managed platforms abstract exactly the layers I wanted to learn, so I removed the abstraction on purpose.","intendedUsers":"Me — and anyone evaluating whether I can operate infrastructure, not just write applications that run on it.","keyFeatures":["Application deployments connected to GitHub repositories","Domain configuration with DNS record management","Reverse proxy and TLS handled through Coolify","Service management — start, stop, logs, environment configuration","Systematic troubleshooting when something refuses to work"],"implementation":["The setup is Coolify on an Ubuntu host: applications deploy from connected GitHub repositories into containers, Coolify's proxy layer terminates TLS and routes configured domains, and DNS records at the registrar point traffic at the host. Environment variables and build configuration live per-application inside Coolify, keeping deployment concerns out of application code.","The workflow is deliberately boring: push to a branch, deploy, watch the logs, verify the domain, fix what's broken. The interesting part is the diagnostic muscle it builds — when a deployment fails, the cause can live anywhere in a chain from DNS propagation to proxy configuration to the application itself, and finding it requires reading each layer honestly."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"DNS is the classic trap: records propagate asynchronously, caches lie, and 'it works for me' is meaningless across resolvers.","solution":"A fixed diagnostic order — registrar, resolver caches, direct DNS queries, then the proxy — plus the discipline to wait out propagation instead of reconfiguring a correct setup out of impatience."},{"challenge":"When a containerised app fails behind a proxy, the error you see is rarely the error that happened.","solution":"Reading logs at every layer in sequence: container stdout, proxy access and error logs, then the application. The proxy's 502 is a symptom; the cause is usually one layer deeper."}],"status":"Ongoing exploration — a working environment I operate continuously, documented as practiced. No fictional pipelines, no invented uptime.","future":["Monitoring and alerting over deployed services","Automated backups with tested restores","Infrastructure expressed as reproducible configuration"]}},{"slug":"tunnel-sharing-utility","name":"Tunnel Sharing Utility","tagline":"Cloudflare Tunnel and ngrok, one command.","summary":"A utility concept for exposing locally hosted applications through configurable tunnel providers — including secure HTTPS access where supported — without touching firewall rules or router configuration.","category":"devops","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Cloudflare Tunnel","ngrok","CLI"],"cover":"pipeline","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["A small utility concept with a narrow job: make local applications shareable. Rather than remembering each provider's flags and credentials, one command starts a tunnel through the provider of your choice — Cloudflare Tunnel, ngrok, or whatever comes next — and hands back the URL.","It exists because local-first development keeps colliding with the reality that demos, webhooks and mobile devices need a public URL. Tunnels solve that; the friction is purely interface."],"problem":"Testing a local app from another device, or receiving webhooks during development, requires a public endpoint. The tools exist but each has its own configuration, auth flow and invocation ritual — and context-switching into them breaks development flow.","intendedUsers":"Developers who need to show or receive traffic for work running on localhost.","keyFeatures":["Provider-configurable tunnels from one interface","Cloudflare Tunnel and ngrok as first-class providers","Secure HTTPS access where the provider supports it","Simple start, stop and status commands"],"implementation":["The design wraps existing tunnel providers rather than reimplementing any tunneling — each provider is a small adapter exposing start, stop and URL retrieval. Provider credentials live in local configuration, and the utility adds the orchestration layer: choosing providers, reporting URLs, and cleaning up processes on exit."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Tunnel processes are easy to start and easy to orphan — background processes that outlive their purpose and hold ports hostage.","solution":"Strict process ownership in the design: the utility tracks what it starts, cleans up on exit, and refuses to pretend a tunnel is gone while its process lives."}],"status":"Concept — built on established tools, contributing ergonomics rather than tunneling.","future":["Configuration profiles per project","Automatic provider failover"]}},{"slug":"media-storage-api","name":"Media Storage API","tagline":"A self-hostable media pipeline, Cloudinary-inspired.","summary":"An API concept for uploading, storing and serving media files through a self-hostable service — owning the upload, storage and delivery path that applications usually rent.","category":"devops","status":"concept","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["REST API","File Storage","Docker"],"cover":"ledger","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["The Media Storage API is a concept for the boring-but-central plumbing of media on the web: an upload endpoint, organised storage, and a delivery path with sensible caching. It is inspired by what Cloudinary offers, deliberately minus the parts that make sense only at scale.","The self-hostable constraint is the point. Media pipelines are where applications quietly surrender sovereignty — files scattered across third-party buckets, URLs that rot when services do. A small, ownable version of that layer is a reasonable thing for any self-hoster to want."],"problem":"Every application that accepts images eventually needs the same trio: accept an upload, keep it somewhere durable, serve it back efficiently. Building it as a standalone service once — rather than per-application — turns repeated glue code into infrastructure you understand completely.","intendedUsers":"Developers self-hosting applications who want a media layer they control, on infrastructure they run.","keyFeatures":["Upload API for media files","Organised storage layout with stable identifiers","Delivery with appropriate cache headers","Self-hostable as a single service"],"implementation":["The concept centres on a clean resource model: media as an addressable resource with metadata (type, size, checksum, timestamps) rather than a filename in a folder. Storage is abstracted behind a small interface — local disk today, anything with the same shape tomorrow — and delivery is HTTP with honest cache semantics, because that is where a media API earns its keep."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Uploads are where APIs are abused — oversized payloads, wrong content types, and filenames that were never meant to be trusted.","solution":"Validation at the boundary: strict size limits, content-type verification against actual bytes, and server-generated identifiers so client-supplied names never reach the filesystem."}],"status":"Concept.","future":["Image transformation on delivery","Deduplication via checksums","Direct-to-storage upload flows"]}},{"slug":"self-hosted-infrastructure","name":"Self-Hosted Development Infrastructure","tagline":"PostgreSQL, Redis, Docker and Linux, run by hand.","summary":"My ongoing exploration of production-style infrastructure — PostgreSQL, Redis, Docker, Linux servers and reverse proxies, operated personally to learn how systems behave when you're the one responsible for them.","category":"devops","status":"ongoing","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["PostgreSQL","Redis","Docker","Linux","Nginx"],"cover":"grid","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["This is the infrastructure counterpart to the Coolify deployment work: a personal environment where I run and operate the services real applications depend on — PostgreSQL for durable data, Redis for caching and queues, Docker for isolation, Nginx as a reverse proxy, all on Linux.","The distinction from a production system is honesty, not effort. This is infrastructure I operate continuously and learn from directly, but it is not serving customers under an SLA — and this portfolio will not pretend otherwise."],"problem":"Abstractions only teach you the abstraction. The problem this work solves is my own: understanding what managed services actually manage — connection behaviour, memory pressure, persistence trade-offs, restart semantics — by being the manager for once.","intendedUsers":"Me, and my projects: every service here backs real applications I run, so the infrastructure is exercised by actual workloads rather than tutorials.","keyFeatures":["PostgreSQL instances serving real application data","Redis for caching and fast-access patterns","Docker for service isolation and reproducibility","Nginx as reverse proxy in front of services","Linux server operation — users, permissions, processes, logs"],"implementation":["Services run as isolated containers where isolation matters (databases especially), with persistent volumes so state survives restarts, and Nginx fronting the services that need HTTP exposure. The operating discipline is the real content: monitoring resource use, reading logs before restarting anything, and changing one variable at a time when something misbehaves."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Redis and PostgreSQL both fail quietly and dramatically in different ways — subtle latency, memory creep, then suddenly a hard stop.","solution":"Learning each service's observability surface before its tuning knobs: what 'healthy' looks like at rest, so deviation is visible early instead of discovered at failure."}],"status":"Ongoing exploration, operated continuously. Described as practiced — not dressed up as production operations.","future":["Backups with tested restoration","Centralised logging across services","Resource monitoring with alerting"]}},{"slug":"aws-ec2-deployment","name":"AWS EC2 Deployment","tagline":"Ubuntu instances in the cloud, managed over SSH.","summary":"Documented experience connecting to Ubuntu EC2 instances over SSH — deploying and managing applications remotely, and learning how cloud servers actually behave when you're the one at the keyboard.","category":"devops","status":"ongoing","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["AWS EC2","SSH","Ubuntu","Linux"],"cover":"pipeline","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["This entry documents my hands-on work with AWS EC2: launching Ubuntu instances, connecting over SSH with key-based authentication, and deploying and managing applications on remote machines.","The cloud, experienced as an operator rather than a dashboard tourist, is a different thing. An EC2 instance is just Linux — until it isn't: security groups behave like a firewall you can't see, the public IP is a fact you must design around, and every long-running process is yours alone to keep alive."],"problem":"Remote servers fail in ways local ones don't: network partitions, key mismanagement, instances that vanish when their billing configuration says so. The problem is building the operational habits — key discipline, explicit configuration, verification after every change — that make remote work reliable instead of lucky.","intendedUsers":"Me and my deployments; anyone assessing whether I can operate cloud infrastructure, not just launch it.","keyFeatures":["SSH key-based access to Ubuntu instances","Remote application deployment and management","Security group configuration for exposed services","Instance lifecycle management — launch, configure, verify"],"implementation":["Access is strictly key-based SSH, with keys and known-hosts managed deliberately rather than opportunistically. Deployment follows the same discipline as local work: explicit steps, verification after each, and logs consulted before assumptions. Security groups are treated as the real firewall they are — every open port is a decision, not a default."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"The gap between 'the command succeeded' and 'the service is reachable' hides most cloud-starter pain — security groups, binding addresses, and public DNS all conspire silently.","solution":"A verification chain that tests locally on the instance first, then through the security group, then from outside — so the failing layer announces itself instead of a vague 'it doesn't work'."}],"status":"Ongoing practical experience — documented as performed, never inflated into 'production cloud operations'.","future":["Infrastructure-as-code for repeatable environments","Automated deployment pipelines on push"]}},{"slug":"alchemist","name":"Alchemist","tagline":"A market-intelligence bot for Telegram.","summary":"A backend-oriented trading intelligence bot combining Telegram, Python and market-structure concepts — BOS, CHOCH, liquidity, fair value gaps and market regimes — as an analysis assistant, never a signal service.","category":"trading","status":"in-development","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"alchemist","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","Telegram Bot API","Market Data APIs"],"cover":"market","featured":true,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["Alchemist is a backend-oriented bot that watches markets and reports structure. Built in Python around Telegram for delivery, it processes price data through technical concepts — break of structure (BOS), change of character (CHOCH), liquidity levels, fair value gaps (FVG) and market regimes — and turns what it observes into readable, source-cited messages.","It is an analysis assistant with strict boundaries: it describes what the market did and where structure sits. It does not predict, promise, or advise — and its messages are worded so that stays true even under pressure."],"problem":"Market-structure analysis is repetitive and mechanical — exactly the work software should absorb. A human analyst scanning pairs for BOS/CHOCH events, liquidity pools and unmitigated gaps is doing pattern detection at machine speed poorly. The problem is automating the observation while leaving every ounce of the judgement with the human.","intendedUsers":"One primary user: me. Alchemist is a personal research tool that surfaces structure across pairs so my own analysis starts informed instead of starting from a blank chart.","keyFeatures":["Structure event detection — BOS and CHOCH","Liquidity level identification","Fair value gap tracking","Market regime labelling","Telegram delivery in plain, non-advisory language"],"implementation":["The pipeline separates three concerns rigidly: data ingestion (market data in), structure analysis (a pure engine turning price series into labelled events), and delivery (Telegram formatting and dispatch). The analysis engine is deliberately stateless-per-scan — every observation derives from data it can re-derive — which makes reports reproducible and bugs localisable.","Terminology discipline is enforced in the delivery layer: the bot reports what happened ('BOS confirmed at level X') and never what to do. That boundary lives in code, not just documentation — advisory phrasing has no template to be rendered from."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Structure detection is noisy: every smoothing choice moves a BOS by candles, and a bot that cries structure constantly trains its user to ignore it.","solution":"Explicit, tunable definitions for every event — the bot reports structure with its parameters visible, so disagreements with the output are resolved by adjusting the definition rather than distrusting the bot."},{"challenge":"Anything adjacent to markets attracts profit-shaped expectations, and tools drift toward feeding them.","solution":"Non-advisory language as a design constraint, no performance claims anywhere in the product, and the project framed everywhere — including here — as analysis software, not a trading system."}],"status":"In development. The structure engine and Telegram delivery are the active work. This is software for market analysis — it is not financial advice, and it makes no performance claims.","future":["Backtesting the structure definitions over history","Additional regime models","User-tunable alert thresholds"]}},{"slug":"trading-market-scanner","name":"Trading Market Scanner","tagline":"Scanning pairs, simulating trades.","summary":"An exploration of market-data integration — cryptocurrency pair discovery, trading simulations and technical analysis — to understand how scanners are actually built, from data to decision surface.","category":"trading","status":"experimental","statusNote":"$undefined","repo":"$undefined","liveUrl":"$undefined","tech":["Python","Market Data APIs"],"cover":"market","featured":false,"published":true,"caseStudy":{"overview":["The Market Scanner is an exploration of the machinery behind trading tools: discovering cryptocurrency pairs, pulling their data, running technical analysis across them, and simulating trades against the results. It is a study of how scanners work — assembled honestly, piece by piece, rather than downloaded as a black box.","The simulation framing matters: simulated trades are a way to test an analysis pipeline against reality without pretending any money was at stake. The scanner's output is insight into its own logic, nothing more."],"problem":"Scanners look like magic and are mostly plumbing: rate limits, pagination, symbol normalisation, indicator maths, and the endless gap between 'data received' and 'data usable'. The problem this project solves is my own understanding of that plumbing — every layer built by hand.","intendedUsers":"Me, as research infrastructure; anyone curious how a market scanner is constructed.","keyFeatures":["Cryptocurrency pair discovery across exchanges","Market-data integration with respectful rate handling","Technical analysis across scanned pairs","Trade simulation against analysis output"],"implementation":["Discovery and scanning are separated: pair discovery produces the universe, scanning evaluates it, and simulation lives behind both. Rate limiting and retry behaviour are first-class — exchanges are the hostile environment every real trading tool must survive — and results carry their timestamps because analysis without time discipline is noise."],"challenges":[{"challenge":"Exchange APIs disagree about everything: naming, precision, pagination, and what a 'pair' even is.","solution":"A normalisation layer with per-exchange adapters, so the analysis engine sees one consistent universe regardless of where the data came from."}],"status":"Experimental. A research exploration of market tooling — no financial advice, no performance claims, no exceptions.","future":["Persisted scan history for comparison","More indicators and configurable scan criteria"]}}]}]]}]
